# `distr::Normal`

The normal (Gaussian) distribution: the bell curve, determined by its mean and standard deviation.

## Signature

```cpp
pluss::Series distr::pdf(dst::Normal, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::logpdf(dst::Normal, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::cdf(dst::Normal, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::logcdf(dst::Normal, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::sample(dst::Normal, const std::vector<double>& params, std::initializer_list<std::int64_t> sizes, pluss::Config cfg = {}, std::uint64_t seed = 0);
```

## What It Does

The normal distribution is the limit of sums of many small independent effects, which is why it models measurement error, heights, and the residuals of well-fitted models. Its density is symmetric about the mean, falls off faster than any exponential in the tails, and is completely described by two numbers: its centre and its spread. All of its cumulants beyond the second are zero, which is the sense in which every other distribution departs from it.

The kind-based functions take `params` as `{}` (the standard normal, mean 0 and standard deviation 1) or `{mean, sd}`. The object form `distr::Normal` also accepts a `Series` for the mean and the standard deviation, so one call evaluates a different normal at every point.

The parameters, in the order of the `params` list:

| Parameter | Constraint | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `mean` | any real | the centre of the distribution; also its median and mode |
| `sd` | > 0 | the standard deviation, the spread of the distribution |

Support: all real numbers.

The object form of this family, with the same functions as methods, is on its own page: [`distr::Normal`](../object_based/Normal.md).

## Parameters

`const pluss::Series& x`
: The points at which to evaluate, a `Series` of any shape; the result has the same shape. Points outside the support have density zero and distribution value 0 or 1.

`const std::vector<double>& params`
: The parameters of the family, in the order shown in the table above (a shorter list selects the defaults: `{}` is the standard normal). A list of the wrong length is refused.

`std::initializer_list<std::int64_t> sizes`
: The shape of the array of random draws, for example `{n}` or `{rows, columns}`.

`pluss::Config cfg`
: The dtype and platform of the draws; the default is Float32 on the CPU, so request Float64 when precision matters.

`std::uint64_t seed`
: The seed of the stream. The same seed always gives the same draws; a seed of 0 is a fixed default, not a random one.

## Returns

`pdf`, `logpdf`, `cdf` and `logcdf` return a `Series` of the shape of `x`; `sample` returns a `Series` of the requested shape.

## Errors

- Parameters outside their valid range are refused with an error that names the requirement (`sd` must be positive).
- A `params` list of the wrong length is refused.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Example 1 evaluates the density (mass) function, the distribution function and the log-density of the Normal on a small grid with `distr::pdf`, `distr::cdf` and `distr::logpdf`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);

  const auto x = pluss::make_series({-4.0, -1.0, 0.0, 1.5, 3.0, 6.0}, f64);
  const std::vector<double> params = {1.5, 2.0};

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (double v : x.items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "pdf:";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Normal, x, params).items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "cdf:";
  for (double v : distr::cdf(dst::Normal, x, params).items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "logpdf:";
  for (double v : distr::logpdf(dst::Normal, x, params).items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
}
```

The density is largest on this grid at x = 1.5, where it is 0.19947; the distribution function runs from 0.00298 at x = -4 to 0.98778 at x = 6, and it is the running total of the density. `logpdf` is the logarithm of the density, for example log(0.19947) = -1.61209 at x = 1.5.

### Example 2

Example 2 checks that the three descriptions of the same probability agree: the integral of the density (sum of the masses) over a range, the difference of the distribution function at its ends, and the fraction of random draws that land in it.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);
  const std::vector<double> params = {1.5, 2.0};

  // The three ways to the probability that the variable lies in [-0.5, 4]:
  const auto grid = pluss::series::linspace(-0.5, 4.0, 3001, f64);
  const double area = as_f64(distr::pdf(dst::Normal, grid, params).antidiff(grid).trapezoid());

  const auto edges = pluss::make_series({-0.5, 4.0}, f64);
  const auto c = distr::cdf(dst::Normal, edges, params).items<double>();

  const auto draws = distr::sample(dst::Normal, params, {200000}, f64, 7ULL);
  const double fraction = as_f64(((draws >= -0.5).to(pluss::ScalarType::Float64) * (draws <= 4.0).to(pluss::ScalarType::Float64)).mean());

  std::cout << "integral of the density: " << area << '\n';
  std::cout << "cdf(4) - cdf(-0.5): " << c[1] - c[0] << '\n';
  std::cout << "fraction of 200000 draws in range: " << fraction << '\n';
}
```

The integral of the density (0.73569) and the difference of the distribution function (0.73569) agree to all printed digits: they are two computations of one probability. The fraction of random draws (0.73606) differs from it by 3.70e-04, which is the size of the sampling error of a fraction of 200000 draws (a standard error of about 9.86e-04), so the density, the distribution function and the sampler describe the same distribution.

### Example 3

Example 3 shows how the shape changes with the parameters: the density at the same points for 3 parameter settings.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({-1.0, 0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 4.0}, f64);

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (double v : x.items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "sd 0.5:";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Normal, x, {1.5, 0.5}).items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "sd 1  :";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Normal, x, {1.5, 1.0}).items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "sd 2  :";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Normal, x, {1.5, 2.0}).items<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
}
```

Read down the rows: `sd 0.5` peaks on this grid at x = 1.5 (value 0.7979); `sd 1` peaks on this grid at x = 1.5 (value 0.3989); `sd 2` peaks on this grid at x = 1.5 (value 0.1995). The parameters move the location, the width and the shape of the curve, which is what choosing a member of the family means.

### Example 4

Example 4 confirms that the standard normal is `{0, 1}` and that a Student t with very many degrees of freedom is indistinguishable from it, by comparing the two densities and distribution functions.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::scientific << std::setprecision(2);
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({-3.0, -1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 3.0}, f64);
  const std::vector<double> here = {0.0, 1.0};
  const std::vector<double> there = {100000.0};

  const double dpdf = as_f64((distr::pdf(dst::Normal, x, here) - distr::pdf(dst::StudentT, x, there)).abs().max());
  const double dcdf = as_f64((distr::cdf(dst::Normal, x, here) - distr::cdf(dst::StudentT, x, there)).abs().max());
  std::cout << "largest difference in the density: " << dpdf << '\n';
  std::cout << "largest difference in the distribution function: " << dcdf << '\n';
}
```

The two largest differences are 1.21e-06 for the density and 1.21e-06 for the distribution function over the grid: small and shrinking as the approximation improves, so the two descriptions agree to the accuracy shown.

